# Running R jobs for @APP@

RStudio gives you one machine for as long as your session lasts. A **batch job**
runs your script on the cluster instead - it can take longer, ask for more CPUs,
and keeps running after you close the browser.

The script here runs your R file with the **same image and the same packages**
as your RStudio session, so a job produces what you see in the console.

## Run it

    cd ~                     # or wherever your script is
    sbatch @COURSEPATH@/run-r-job.sh my_script.R

Your output lands in the folder you submitted from, named `@APP@-<jobid>.out`:

    squeue -u $USER          # is it still running?
    cat @APP@-12345.out      # what did it print?

Submit from a folder of your own. This one is read-only to you, so a job started
from inside it cannot write its output.

## Ask for more

You do not need to edit anything. Options on the command line beat the settings
in the script:

    sbatch -c 4 -t 02:00:00 -J hw3 @COURSEPATH@/run-r-job.sh my_script.R

`-c` CPUs, `-t` time as `HH:MM:SS`, `-J` a name of your own so you can pick your
job out of `squeue`.

**Order matters.** Options go *before* the script name. Put them after it and
they are handed to your R script instead: the job still runs, the option is
quietly ignored, and nothing tells you. This does not do what it looks like:

    sbatch @COURSEPATH@/run-r-job.sh my_script.R -J hw3      # -J ignored

## Make it your own

Only if you need to change what the script *does* - a different image, extra
setup. Copy it first; your copy is yours and nothing will overwrite it:

    cp @COURSEPATH@/run-r-job.sh my-run.sh

Your copy still reads this course's image and library from `course-env.sh` at
run time, so it will not go stale when the course changes.

## If something breaks

The copy in @COURSEPATH@ is the class version and is always current. Copy it
again and you are back to a known-good starting point.
